hi there steve kaufman here today i want  to talk about conjugating spanish verbs  uh why because i googled to see what  were the main problems people had with  spanish various websites discuss the  difficulties of different languages it's  amazing how we can find so much  information just by googling  and conjugation how to conjugate spanish  verbs was the biggest problem people at  least  mentioned and then they talked about you  know  ser and the star  poor para  words that in english seem to have the  same meaning but in spanish there's two  different uh words  and then they mention pronouns they  meant to mention subjunctives but  conjugation seemed to be the biggest  problem  and i guess there's two aspects to  the conjugation of spanish verbs one is  the concept for an english speaker  the idea that the ending of a verb  changes for each person  and that the form changes for tense  we have some of that in english but our  uh verbs tend not to change verb endings  tend not to change according to the  person in spanish they do and that's an  a reason why in spanish very often you  don't have to  provide the pronoun  because  the verb already tells you who is doing  it but the issue is is not so much  understanding that  accepting the fact that the verbs change  the issue is trying to remember the  endings all right  so  uh  there are different websites that  suggest different ways of memorizing the  uh endings of spanish verbs  the problem is that these uh conjugation  tables are huge because there are so  many different tenses and in each tense  the endings change according to the  person  so  my advice in learning conjugation the  conjugation of spanish verbs  is no different really than advice when  it comes to learning  a list of any information like the  colors or the days of the week  uh  or even counting  parts of the body  uh you can  every so often uh you know glance over a  list of these things conjugation tables  uh declension tables in those languages  where nouns and adjectives change  according to case  you can glance over them you can read  them in my experience you can think  you're learning them you can think  you've memorized them  and yet  you forget them you forget them very  soon after having worked very hard  trying to learn them and particularly  under the pressure of speaking when  you're trying to get sort of some  momentum and some rhythm in the language  to try and nail the correct  ending  uh  by trying to go through what you  memorized  is very difficult  so what i suggest people do  is that my feeling is you have to get  you have to get used to  the way the language is spoken you have  to get used to these endings so you  start to produce them naturally  and the proof that you know a lot of  instruction doesn't help  can be found in english  and so many non-native speakers who have  had 10 years of english at school  still say he do  instead if he does  in other words or maybe they get that he  does but then in some other situation  the third person singular which in  english takes the s all of a sudden  loses the s because they haven't  developed  enough of a  of a habit of saying it correctly  we can even see this in native speakers  of english  who have had you know um  schooling for six seven eight nine ten  eleven twelve years probably have had  their essays corrected  probably have used english correctly  but  uh you know i'll take an example i was  in northern alberta  at a very small primitive sawmill in a  small town and it was just i was buying  lumber from them for export to germany  but the equipment was very rudimentary  and  and and the their employee there had to  go out in 30 below zero weather and pick  up four  you know  squares four by four squares pick them  up and move them to another pile and he  did this all day long  and i said to the owner there or maybe  was the guy supervising them i said like  how can you get those people to do that  every day  all day in 30 below zero weather and the  guy's reply was he don't know no better  he don't know no better this is a native  speaker  native speaker probably didn't have a  lot of schooling  but  while in school probably he was taught  how to say he doesn't but be why does he  say he don't know no better because  that's what he hears around him  that's what he's picked up on  that's why people today are starting to  say  i would have went  instead of i would have gone i would  have came instead of i would have come  so  what we are surrounded with what we hear  all the time what we read oh if we read  this is what forms our language habits  so when it comes to  spanish conjugations  uh there is a tremendous dictionary i  mean there are other dictionaries but  there are so there are conjugating  dictionaries so when i'm on link for  example i looked up spanish on link and  people are going to get mad at me for  mentioning link but that's where i learn  languages that's my language learning  environment  and so i pick a verb  and i can choose con context reverso  conjugator so if i do that so if i see  a verb  and then i go to context reversal i see  the meaning but i see the full  conjugation of that verb so i go for the  one that i  am interested in or it could have been  uh something else in the future  whatever it might be i go and find it to  confirm to my mind  what that particular form of the verb is  and so doing a kind of glance around the  others don't try to nail it down and i  go back to the text of what i'm reading  and i also listen to that  so the reference to the table the  reference to the conjugation table  is part of the process of acquisition it  has a minor role i think we we  overestimate the degree to which we're  able to retain  what we  acquire while reading the table but it  is sort of satisfying to read the table  and you kind of refresh your mind a  little bit about different forms of the  verb that you have seen  but then you got to go back to the  context  read listen  uh notice verbs  while you're listening while you're  reading  and do as much listening and reading as  possible  and when in doubt about a verb you know  what is the  particular form here look it up look it  up a bit like the dictionary get in and  out  have a look at it look at some of the  others come back to your um  text  don't be too ambitious don't think  you're going to nail anything down and  if you keep on doing this  and if you give yourself enough exposure  you will gradually get accustomed to  certain phrases certain ways of using  the language which will be increasingly  correct including  uh being able to use on the fly the  perfect tense the future tense the  conditional tense because it'll be  within a context of meaning that you  have gotten used to so that's my advice  on learning the conjugation of spanish  verbs don't try to master it don't try  to nail it down  in my experience that's futile  refer to it occasionally and spend most  of your time  getting accustomed to the language okay  there you have it my take on the how to  learn the conjugation of spanish  verbs bye for now  [Music]  you
 

